# Mastra Conventions

## Overview

Mastra is the project's AI runtime. It talks to an OpenAI-compatible AI
gateway (sluis.ai, a self-hosted LiteLLM proxy, or any compatible endpoint —
configured via `NUXT_AI_GATEWAY_URL`) through the AI SDK's OpenAI-compatible
provider; agents reference models with the raw gateway model id
(`openai/gpt-4o-mini`, `vertex_ai/gemini-3.1-flash`, etc). No prefix
juggling in agent code.

The runtime singleton (`server/mastra/index.ts`) wires `PostgresStore` for
thread/memory persistence and `Observability` so Mastra Studio shows
conversation history and traces.

## Packages

- `@mastra/core`: Mastra instance and Agent definitions
- `@mastra/ai-sdk`: `handleChatStream()` for Nuxt server routes
- `@mastra/loggers`: `PinoLogger`
- `@mastra/pg`: `PostgresStore` for threads, `PgVector` for RAG
- `@mastra/observability`: `Observability` + `DefaultExporter`
- `@ai-sdk/vue`: `Chat` class for Vue components
- `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`: AI gateway transport
- `ai`: Vercel AI SDK primitives

## Structure

- Runtime singleton: `server/mastra/index.ts`
- Agents: `server/mastra/agents/`
- Utils: `server/mastra/utils/` (`model-config.ts`, `ai-model.ts`)
- Chat / RAG endpoints call `mastra.getAgent(...)` rather than instantiating their own clients

Use **relative imports** inside `server/mastra/`; the standalone `mastra dev`
bundler can't resolve Nuxt's `#server/*` alias.

## Defining Agents

Resolve model + instructions at call time from the agent's `agents` table row
via `getAgentModelId(agentKey)` and `getAgentInstructions(agentKey)`
(`server/mastra/utils/agent-runtime.ts`). The row links the agent to an
`ai_model_configs.key` and (optionally) a `prompts.key`; admins repoint either
from `/dashboard/settings/ai`. Both resolvers fall back to env / code defaults
when a row, prompt, or table is absent. The returned model id is already
`gateway/`-prefixed so Mastra routes through `OpenAICompatGateway` (registered
in `server/mastra/index.ts`) instead of its built-in provider lookups; those
would demand per-provider API keys (OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, …)
and deselect the model when the key is missing.

```ts
import { Agent } from '@mastra/core/agent'
import { getAgentModelId, getAgentInstructions } from '../utils/agent-runtime'

export const myAgent = new Agent({
    id: 'my-agent',
    name: 'My Agent',
    instructions: () => getAgentInstructions('my-agent'),
    model: () => getAgentModelId('my-agent'),
})
```

Add the agent to the Mastra constructor in `server/mastra/index.ts` and give it
an entry in `server/mastra/agents/registry.ts` (name, default model config +
prompt). On boot, `server/plugins/10-sync-ai-on-boot.ts` registers a DB row for
every agent on the Mastra instance: insert-if-missing, never deletes. For a
fixed system prompt with no admin control, pass an `instructions` string
instead and leave `promptKey` null in the registry.

Use `getActiveModelId(key)` directly only for non-agent model lookups (it backs
`getAgentModelId`).

Embeddings still go through `getEmbeddingModel()` from
`server/mastra/utils/model-config.ts`: Mastra gateways resolve chat models,
not embedding models.

Register agents in `server/mastra/index.ts`:

```ts
import { Mastra } from '@mastra/core'
import { myAgent } from './agents/my-agent'

export const mastra = new Mastra({
    agents: { myAgent },
    storage,
    logger,
    observability,
})
```

## Server Route (HTTP streaming)

```ts
import { handleChatStream } from '@mastra/ai-sdk'
import { createUIMessageStreamResponse } from 'ai'
import { mastra } from '../../mastra'

export default defineEventHandler(async (event) => {
    const body = await readBody(event)
    const stream = await handleChatStream({
        mastra,
        agentId: 'my-agent',
        params: body,
    })
    return createUIMessageStreamResponse({ stream })
})
```

## Frontend Chat Component

```ts
import { Chat } from '@ai-sdk/vue'
import { DefaultChatTransport } from 'ai'

const chat = new Chat({
    transport: new DefaultChatTransport({ api: '/api/chat' }),
})
```

Use `chat.messages`, `chat.sendMessage({ text })`, and `chat.status` in templates.

## Best Practices

- Keep agent instructions clear and focused
- Use the cheapest model that gets the job done
- Register every agent in `server/mastra/index.ts`
- Call `mastra.getAgent(...)` in server routes; never call agents from the frontend
- Model ids are raw gateway strings: `openai/gpt-4o-mini`, `vertex_ai/gemini-3.1-flash`. No `custom/` or `gateway/` prefix.
- `battlestack mastra:studio` runs Mastra Studio (dev only): port allocated per project
